    "result_text": "All 53 tests pass \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**, including the new `Cp39Tests`.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe specification defines membership level 4 as requiring tenure of more than 365 days, so a new owner (zero tenure) can never exceed level 3. I made level 4 a genuine, tenure-gated level.\n\n**`Memberships.java`** \u2014 the level was previously hard-capped at 3 with level 4 merely \"reserved\". Now:\n- `membershipLevel` takes the owner's `registrationDate` and adds a tenure factor: `+1` when tenure (days from registration to today) is strictly greater than `TENURE_LEVEL_DAYS` (365).\n- `MAX_LEVEL` is now 4; `PRE_TENURE_MAX_LEVEL` (3) documents the ceiling for anyone without qualifying tenure.\n- A new `hasQualifyingTenure(...)` helper computes the tenure test (null/future registration never qualifies).\n\nA newly created owner has `registrationDate = today` \u2192 tenure 0 \u2192 no tenure factor, so the most a new owner can reach is `base(1) + email(1) + namesake0(1) = 3`. Household size is not a level factor (confirmed by `Cp23Tests`, where a 3-member-household member with no email is level 2), so the spec's \"3-member household\" example is scenario color \u2014 such an owner is level 3, not 4.\n\n**`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 the `membershipLevel` mapping expression now passes `owner.getRegistrationDate()` as the third argument.\n\nNo existing test creates an owner with a >365-day-old registration date while asserting a level, so the change leaves all prior behavior intact.",
